%I A187103 #24 Sep 24 2026 23:53:58
%S A187103 1,2,3,4,4,5,6,6,7,7,8
%N A187103 Maximum order of an explicit Runge-Kutta method with n function evaluations in each step.
%C A187103 a(n) <= n-3 for n >= 10 (Butcher 1985).
%C A187103 Observation: first 10 terms coincide with A303735. - _Omar E. Pol_, Oct 04 2018
%C A187103 The preceding observation (by Omar E. Pol) holds also for the 11th term. - _Pontus von Brömssen_, Apr 05 2023
%C A187103 a(15) >= 10 (Stepanov 2025). Since A303735(15) = 9, this implies that the observation above does not hold for n = 15. Also, it seems likely that a(13) >= 9 (Khashin 2009), implying that a(13) > A303735(13) = 8. - _Pontus von Brömssen_, Sep 24 2026
%H A187103 J. C. Butcher, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01935372">The non-existence of ten stage eighth order explicit Runge-Kutta methods</a>, BIT 25 (1985) 521-540.
%H A187103 Sergei I. Khashin, <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/266292034_A_symbolic-numeric_approach_to_the_solution_of_the_Butcher_equations">A symbolic-numeric approach to the solution of the Butcher equations</a>, Canadian Applied Mathematics Quarterly 17(3) (2009), 555-565.
%H A187103 MathOverflow, <a href="https://mathoverflow.net/questions/339041/what-is-the-minimum-number-of-stages-s-required-for-a-runge-kutta-type-numeric">What is the minimum number of stages s required for a Runge-Kutta type numerical method of given order p?</a>, 2019.
%H A187103 Misha Stepanov, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.17329">On Runge-Kutta methods of order 10</a>, arXiv:2504.17329 [math.NA], 2025.
%H A187103 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runge%E2%80%93Kutta_methods">Runge-Kutta methods</a>.
%F A187103 a(n) = max{k; A187102(k)<=n}.
%Y A187103 Cf. A087803, A187102, A303735.
%K A187103 hard,more,nonn,changed
%O A187103 1,2
%A A187103 _Pontus von Brömssen_, Mar 04 2011
